h('10. DBMS design and integrity mechanisms')
sub('10.1 Constraints and normalization')
p('Checks reject negative balances, nonpositive transfers, self-transfers, invalid states, invalid scores and invalid coordinates. Snapshot arithmetic requires source_after = source_before - amount and destination_after = destination_before + amount. Composite account/customer and device/customer FKs enforce ownership. Ledger checks enforce positive sequence and amount, nonnegative balances and side-specific arithmetic. A VALID certificate requires 0 &lt;= lower &lt;= upper &lt;= target; INCOMPLETE requires NULL bounds and no review flag.')
p('Customers, accounts, devices, predictions and alerts separate distinct facts. transaction.customer_id and alert.transaction_id are deliberate navigation denormalizations, constrained by composite FKs. Therefore the design should not be described as strictly pure 3NF throughout. Evidence membership resolves the many-to-many reuse of ledger entries by certificates; JSON captures versioned event evidence rather than replacing relational identity.')
sub('10.2 Triggers, views and SQL functions')
table([['Object','Purpose'],['prediction_alert trigger','After prediction insertion, creates an OPEN alert if persisted risk reaches the database threshold'],['Audit triggers','Capture redacted INSERT / UPDATE / DELETE history for customers, accounts, transfers, predictions and alerts'],['Evidence protection triggers','Reject updates/deletes to retained ledger/certificate evidence; protect certified financial facts'],['suspicious_transactions_view','Joins customer, transfer, prediction and optional alert'],['customer_risk_summary','Aggregated customer counts, amounts, risk and suspicious activity'],['flow_certificate_detail_view','Joins certificate, ordered evidence membership and ledger facts'],['PostgreSQL SQL functions','calculate_transaction_velocity and get_customer_transaction_stats']],[166,321])
sub('10.3 Indexes and migrations')
p('Important indexes cover customer/time and account/time transaction history, prediction risk, alert status, ledger(account_id, recorded_at, seq), and certificate(review, status). PK/unique indexes support identity and retry checks. Use EXPLAIN rather than assuming an index improves tiny workloads. Alembic revisions create the base schema, database objects (0002), flow evidence (0003) and reporting/protection (0004).')
sub('10.4 ACID and retention')
p('Atomicity comes from one transaction; constraints and service checks preserve consistency; ordered row locks coordinate cooperating PostgreSQL writers; committed records persist across connections. Reversals are new opposite-direction movements. The local FABC run did not verify PostgreSQL restart recovery or new PostgreSQL trigger execution. Direct monetary writers must follow the protocol.')
page()
h('11. Machine learning, graphs and explainability')
sub('11.1 Dataset and training pipeline')
p('The offline pipeline uses PaySim columns such as step, type, amount, sender/receiver IDs, original/destination balances and isFraud. It validates and preprocesses data, engineers transaction features, trains Logistic Regression, Random Forest and XGBoost variants, compares models, tunes thresholds, assigns risk bands and saves explanations. Raw data and fitted artifacts are not committed; a compatible trusted baseline artifact is required for online transfers.')
sub('11.2 Transaction and graph features')
p('Transaction features describe amount, type, balance changes, account draining and related arithmetic. A directed graph models sender_account to receiver_account. Node features include in/out degree, sent/received totals, transaction counts and averages. Edge features include pair transaction count, total amount and average amount. The original full-graph experiments also contain label-derived fraud rates and may include target/future information; their scores are unsuitable as evidence of leakage-free generalization.')
p('A separate historical implementation uses no fraud-label aggregates. All transactions in one hourly PaySim step observe the same graph built from strictly earlier steps. The graph is updated only after that whole step is processed. The chronological comparison chooses thresholds on validation data and reports a later held-out test set. It does not establish ten-minute FABC quality because PaySim time is hourly.')
sub('11.3 Online risk contract')
p('The baseline inference adapter constructs PaySim-compatible balance features from a live transfer and loads the existing fitted sklearn pipeline. It records model name and artifact SHA-256 version. The local mapping uses UTC hour-of-day plus one for step, unlike elapsed PaySim hours; this is an integration/domain limitation. Online graph features require a separate adapter and are not inferred automatically.')
p('SQL behaviour features include recent transfer counts, historical average amount, deviation from average, previous-transfer interval, device trust and location changes. Weighted rules yield a bounded behaviour score. This is a heuristic, not a calibrated probability. ML probability, behaviour score and combined risk remain separately stored.')
sub('11.4 Outputs and evaluation')
p('Offline evaluation includes precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC, average precision and confusion matrices. Risk bands help prioritize review. Global feature importance and transaction-level rule explanations aid interpretation; they do not prove causation and are not an implemented SHAP attribution system. The dashboard supports comparisons and investigation of saved artifacts.')
page()
h('12. FABC: exact recent-funding bounds')
p('FABC asks how much of a target outgoing payment must, and could, be funded by credits inside the source account\'s ten-minute observation window. It accounts for opening funds and every intervening debit. Bounds are exact under complete ordered evidence, one currency, no overdrafts and no unrecorded balance changes. They describe feasible allocations, not an observable unique funds identity.')
sub('12.1 Linear Decimal algorithm')
p('Let B be the current balance, initially the opening balance. L and U are the minimum and maximum recent-funded amounts that can remain in the account. Initially L = U = 0. Before applying a debit, use the pre-debit B.')
code('Credit c:\n    B = B + c\n    L = L + c\n    U = U + c\nEarlier debit d:\n    L = max(0, L - d)\n    U = min(U, B - d)\n    B = B - d\nTarget debit x:\n    lower_amount = max(0, x - (B - L))\n    upper_amount = min(x, U)\n    lower_share = lower_amount / x\n    upper_share = upper_amount / x')
p('Spending recent funds first at prior debits minimizes the recent balance left; spending old funds first maximizes it. These feasible extremes propagate through the ordered history. The scan uses O(n) arithmetic and O(1) working state; retaining the full evidence requires O(n) storage. Marginal bounds for different payments cannot be summed as though their endpoints were jointly attainable.')
sub('12.2 Worked example: unavoidable recent funding')
table([['Event (INR)','Balance B','Recent balance [L, U]'],['Opening balance 1,000','1,000','[0, 0]'],['Credit 4,000 from A','5,000','[4,000, 4,000]'],['Credit 3,000 from B','8,000','[7,000, 7,000]'],['Credit 2,000 from C','10,000','[9,000, 9,000]'],['Earlier debit 2,000','8,000','[7,000, 8,000]'],['Target debit 7,500','Pre-target: 8,000','Target interval: [6,500, 7,500]']],[190,100,197])
p('The minimum share is 86.67%, with three incoming counterparties, so the fixed demo policy requests review. With opening funds of INR 100,000, recent receipts of INR 9,000 and a target of INR 7,500, the interval instead becomes [0, 7,500]: old funds alone could cover the entire payment. Neither example determines fraud.')
page()
h('13. Certificate persistence, replay and failure handling')
sub('13.1 Evidence selection and completeness')
p('The service reads the source prefix before the target DEBIT. A ten-minute inclusive start identifies recent credits; a preceding ledger entry provides an opening anchor, or a known opening state is used when valid. Per-account sequences and before/after arithmetic are checked for continuity. Server-recorded nondecreasing timestamps govern observation, so caller timestamps cannot reorder the funding evidence.')
p('The scan cap is 10,000 history entries. Missing anchors, balance/sequence gaps, an unobserved legacy period or a capped history produce INCOMPLETE, NULL bounds and review=false. Migration preserves old transfers without inventing retrospective certificates. A migrated account can remain incomplete until a full observation window is available.')
sub('13.2 Stored certificate')
p('A certificate stores target identity, source account, target sequence/amount, status, bounds, review flag, versions, a canonical payload and SHA-256 digest. certificate_evidence records ordered membership with OPENING_ANCHOR, HISTORY and TARGET roles. Money is represented as fixed two-decimal strings and timestamps as UTC in the payload. Retention triggers reject evidence mutation; certified transfer financial facts are protected.')
sub('13.3 Replay')
p('Replay checks the certificate identity, versions, digest, membership ordering, live ledger facts and recomputed bounds/results. A matching result shows agreement with retained inputs and implementation. It does not authenticate the original source data or defeat a privileged administrator who rewrites the data and digest together. Backups, restricted database roles and external trust controls remain necessary.')
sub('13.4 Separate analyst review')
p('The fixed funding policy requests review when lower_share is at least 0.80 and at least three distinct incoming accounts appear in the ten-minute window. These are demo parameters, not regulatory thresholds. The Banking page provides certificate inspection, replay and a funding-review queue. An incomplete result is visible uncertainty, not a numerical zero.')
sub('13.5 API and transaction guarantees')
code('POST /transactions  (optional idempotency_key)\nGET /transactions/{id}/flow-certificate\nGET /transactions/{id}/flow-certificate/replay\nGET /fraud/flow-reviews')
p('Certificates are generated before ordinary prediction insertion inside the same transaction. Failure after certificate writes must roll back all associated movement and prediction state. A transfer predating the migration has no certificate and inspection returns 404. Two-entry conservation relies on the transfer service and cooperating writers; a row CHECK cannot enforce the full cross-row protocol.')
page()
h('14. Offline joint-funding research prototype')
sub('14.1 Collective bounds')
p('The joint prototype builds a chronological capacity network for one source cohort, one currency and complete ordered internal movements plus supported external credits. Opening accounts and external credits carry independently supported source-attribution intervals. The optimizer minimizes and maximizes the cohort amount held across selected final accounts. Internal transfers move capacity; they do not inject a new source.')
p('Example: INR 10,000 of selected-source funds mixes with INR 10,000 of other funds and ends in two accounts holding INR 10,000 each. Each account separately can contain [0, 10,000] from the source, but the two together must hold exactly INR 10,000. Joint conservation captures the dependency lost by adding independent marginal intervals. Targets are final holdings, not arbitrary serial transfers.')
sub('14.2 Certificate and independent verification')
p('Monetary values use integer minor units. Output includes lower/upper bounds, a threshold condition (MET, NOT_MET or UNRESOLVED), endpoint flow witnesses, version/snapshot metadata and a digest. A separate verifier checks capacity, conservation and optimality using the absence of negative residual cycles, without rerunning the optimizer. It still depends on trustworthy input evidence.')
sub('14.3 Decision-directed evidence selection')
p('Each query addresses an obtainable boundary record with positive acquisition cost. Its possible integer outcome intervals must partition the current boundary interval. The prototype evaluates outcomes, ranks guaranteed decision-ambiguity reduction per cost, and can apply a chosen outcome to a copy of the ledger before fully recomputing bounds. Selection is deterministic and greedy; useful combinations of individually unhelpful queries may be missed.')
